IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `claq-common`
IMPORT `claq-liability`

-- Civil Liability Act 2003 (Queensland) -- worked cases for the claimant's questions in claq-common.l4 (s 5) and
-- claq-liability.l4 (chapter 2; chapter 4, parts 1 and 1A). Every expected value is worked by hand from the deposited
-- text, with the section, never copied from the encoding's output.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases: does the Act reach my claim, and liability`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 5`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Property damage: s 5 speaks only of personal injury, so the Act applies (s 4(1)).
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `damage to property` `compensation is payable under the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 for an injury, other than one to which s 34(1)(c) or s 35 of that Act applies` mesothelioma FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to my claim for damages for harm (s 4(1))`
-- A personal injury with no workers' compensation, no dust, no tobacco.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `no workers' compensation is payable for any of my injuries` `another injury or condition` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to my claim for damages for harm (s 4(1))`
-- s 5(1)(b): compensation payable under the 2003 Act (claimed or not, s 5(2)).
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `compensation is payable under the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 for an injury, other than one to which s 34(1)(c) or s 35 of that Act applies` `another injury or condition` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Act does not apply to my personal injury claim, because workers' compensation is payable for an injury (s 5(1)(a) or (b)); whether I claim it is immaterial (s 5(2))`
-- s 5(1)(b) "other than an injury to which section 34(1)(c) or 35 of that Act applies".
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `compensation is payable under the Workers' Compensation and Rehabilitation Act 2003 only for an injury to which s 34(1)(c) or s 35 of that Act applies` `another injury or condition` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to my claim for damages for harm (s 4(1))`
-- s 5(1)(a), the 1996 Act, both ways.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `compensation is payable under the WorkCover Queensland Act 1996 for an injury, other than one to which s 36(1)(c) or s 37 of that Act applies` `another injury or condition` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the Act does not apply to my personal injury claim, because workers' compensation is payable for an injury (s 5(1)(a) or (b)); whether I claim it is immaterial (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `compensation is payable under the WorkCover Queensland Act 1996 only for an injury to which s 36(1)(c) or s 37 of that Act applies` `another injury or condition` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to my claim for damages for harm (s 4(1))`
-- s 5(1)(c), a listed disease (paragraph (a) of the definition), and an unlisted lung condition attributable to dust
-- (paragraph (b)), and one not attributable to dust.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `no workers' compensation is payable for any of my injuries` mesothelioma FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no, except section 59A damages: my injury is or includes a dust-related condition (s 5(1)(c), (3))`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `no workers' compensation is payable for any of my injuries` `farmers' lung` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no, except section 59A damages: my injury is or includes a dust-related condition (s 5(1)(c), (3))`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `no workers' compensation is payable for any of my injuries` `another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no, except section 59A damages: my injury is or includes a dust-related condition (s 5(1)(c), (3))`
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `no workers' compensation is payable for any of my injuries` `another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the Act applies to my claim for damages for harm (s 4(1))`
-- s 5(1)(d), tobacco.
#ASSERT `Does the Act apply to my claim` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `no workers' compensation is payable for any of my injuries` `another condition of the lung, pleura or peritoneum` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no, except section 59A damages: my injury results from smoking or other use of tobacco products or exposure to tobacco smoke (s 5(1)(d), (3))`
#ASSERT `sch 2 -- a dust-related condition` silicosis FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`sch 2 -- a dust-related condition` `another injury or condition` TRUE)

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 9`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can the court find a breach of duty` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 9 does not stop the court finding a breach: the risk was foreseeable, not insignificant, and a reasonable person would have taken the precautions; the court decides`
#ASSERT `Can the court find a breach of duty` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no breach: the risk was not foreseeable (s 9(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can the court find a breach of duty` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no breach: the risk was insignificant (s 9(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Can the court find a breach of duty` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no breach: a reasonable person in the defendant's position would not have taken the precautions (s 9(1)(c))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 13-16`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- An obvious risk, nothing in s 15(2): no duty to warn.
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `not a professional` FALSE EQUALS `no: the defendant owed me no duty to warn of an obvious risk (s 15(1))`
-- s 13(5): the go-cart with a hidden crack: not obvious, so s 15 does not apply.
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE `not a professional` FALSE EQUALS `s 15 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
-- s 13(5) "unless the failure itself is an obvious risk".
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE `not a professional` FALSE EQUALS `no: the defendant owed me no duty to warn of an obvious risk (s 15(1))`
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `not a professional` FALSE EQUALS `s 15 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
-- s 15(2)(a), (b), (c).
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `not a professional` FALSE EQUALS `s 15(1) does not apply because I asked the defendant for advice or information about the risk (s 15(2)(a)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `not a professional` FALSE EQUALS `s 15(1) does not apply because a written law required the defendant to warn me (s 15(2)(b)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `a professional other than a doctor (a person practising a profession)` TRUE EQUALS `s 15(1) does not apply because the defendant is a professional, not a doctor, and the risk was of my death or personal injury from their professional service (s 15(2)(c)); no duty to warn is presumed (s 15(3)), the general law decides`
-- s 15(2)(c) "other than a doctor".
#ASSERT `Was I owed a warning of an obvious risk` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `a doctor` TRUE EQUALS `no: the defendant owed me no duty to warn of an obvious risk (s 15(1))`
-- s 14.
#ASSERT `Am I taken to have known of the risk` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: I am taken to have been aware of the risk, unless I prove I was not (s 14(1))`
#ASSERT `Am I taken to have known of the risk` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: I have proved on the balance of probabilities that I was not aware of the risk, even of its type or kind (s 14)`
#ASSERT `Am I taken to have known of the risk` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 14 does not apply: the defendant has not raised voluntary assumption of risk`
#ASSERT `Am I taken to have known of the risk` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 14 does not apply: the risk was not an obvious risk (s 13)`
-- s 16.
#ASSERT `Does an inherent risk bar my claim` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no liability in negligence: the harm came from an inherent risk, one that could not be avoided by reasonable care and skill (s 16(1))`
#ASSERT `Does an inherent risk bar my claim` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 16 does not bar my claim that I should have been warned of the risk (s 16(3))`
#ASSERT `Does an inherent risk bar my claim` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 16 does not apply: the harm did not come from an inherent risk`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 17-19`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no liability in negligence: the harm came from an obvious risk of a dangerous recreational activity I engaged in, whether or not I was aware of it (s 19)`
#ASSERT `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 19 does not apply: my claim is not in negligence (s 17(1))`
#ASSERT `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 19 does not apply: the activity was not a dangerous recreational activity (s 18)`
#ASSERT `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 19 does not apply: the activity was not a dangerous recreational activity (s 18)`
-- The bungee cord used past its replacement time (s 13(5), example 2): not an obvious risk.
#ASSERT `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 19 does not apply: the harm did not come from an obvious risk of the activity materialising`
#ASSERT `Does a dangerous recreational activity bar my claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 19 does not apply: the harm did not come from an obvious risk of the activity materialising`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 21, 22`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Did my doctor breach the duty to warn me` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no breach of the duty to warn: the doctor gave me (or arranged) both kinds of information about the risk (s 21(1))`
#ASSERT `Did my doctor breach the duty to warn me` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 21 does not stop a finding of breach: the doctor did not give the information a reasonable person in my position would require (s 21(1)(a))`
#ASSERT `Did my doctor breach the duty to warn me` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 21 does not stop a finding of breach: the doctor did not give information the doctor knew or ought reasonably to have known I wanted (s 21(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no breach: the professional acted in a way widely accepted by peer professional opinion as competent practice (s 22(1))`
#ASSERT `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `s 22 gives no defence on these facts: the court considers the peer professional opinion irrational or contrary to a written law (s 22(2))`
#ASSERT `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 22 gives no defence on these facts: it is not established that the professional acted as widely accepted peer professional opinion holds competent`
#ASSERT `Does peer professional opinion defend the professional` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 22 does not apply to a claim about a warning, advice or information about the risk of harm (s 22(5))`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 26, 27; Regulation sch 1`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Babcock Mission Critical Services Australasia Pty Ltd when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `another entity or person, not prescribed in sch 1` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given by or for an entity prescribed by the Civil Liability Regulation 2025`
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Babcock Mission Critical Services Australasia Pty Ltd when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given in circumstances of emergency`
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Babcock Mission Critical Services Australasia Pty Ltd when providing aeromedical, air rescue or other emergency helicopter services under an agreement with the State (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the act was not done in good faith, or was done with reckless disregard for safety`
-- Sch 1 prescribes 21 entities, not one: an ambulance officer, a council, a surf life saver, the last in the list.
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Queensland Ambulance Service established under the Ambulance Service Act 1991 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `a local government or joint local government under the Local Government Act 2009 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Surf Life Saving Queensland and affiliated bodies (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Volunteer Marine Rescue Assoc Qld Inc and affiliated bodies (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: civil liability does not attach to the aid given in an emergency, in good faith and without reckless disregard, by or for a prescribed public safety entity (ss 26, 27)`
-- A prescribed entity's person, but not performing public safety duties for it (s 26(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `Can I sue for emergency aid given to me` `Queensland Ambulance Service established under the Ambulance Service Act 1991 (sch 1), or a person performing duties for it` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ss 26 and 27 do not apply: the aid was not given by or for an entity prescribed by the Civil Liability Regulation 2025`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ch 2 pt 2`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- A business's claim for economic loss for negligent advice: apportionable.
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `economic loss` `a breach of a duty of care` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: my claim is an apportionable claim; each defendant who is a concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for its just share (s 31), and I am to make the claim against everyone I have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `damage to property` `misleading or deceptive conduct (Australian Consumer Law (Queensland) s 18, under the Fair Trading Act 1989)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: my claim is an apportionable claim; each defendant who is a concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for its just share (s 31), and I am to make the claim against everyone I have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `personal injury (including death, pre-natal injury, psychological or psychiatric injury, or disease)` `a breach of a duty of care` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: proportionate liability is only for claims for economic loss or damage to property (s 28(1))`
-- Economic loss arising out of personal injury (s 28(3)(a)).
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `economic loss` `a breach of a duty of care` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim arising out of personal injury (s 28(3)(a))`
-- A consumer: an individual, goods for household use (s 29(a)); and professional advice for personal use (s 29(b)).
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `damage to property` `a breach of a duty of care` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim by a consumer (s 28(3)(b), s 29)`
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `economic loss` `a breach of a duty of care` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: proportionate liability does not apply to a claim by a consumer (s 28(3)(b), s 29)`
-- A company is not an individual, so not a consumer.
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `economic loss` `a breach of a duty of care` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: my claim is an apportionable claim; each defendant who is a concurrent wrongdoer is liable only for its just share (s 31), and I am to make the claim against everyone I have reasonable grounds to believe may be liable (s 32(1))`
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `economic loss` `something else` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the claim is not for breach of a duty of care or under the Australian Consumer Law s 18 (s 28(1))`
#ASSERT `Is my claim apportionable` `economic loss` `a breach of a duty of care` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: an Act makes the liability joint and several (s 28(4))`
-- s 31: loss 100,000, my share 20%, defendant 40% of the rest: 100,000 x 80% x 40% = 32,000.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can recover from this wrongdoer` 100000 20 40 FALSE EQUALS 32000
-- ss 32D-32F: severally liable for the others too: 100,000 x 80% = 80,000.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can recover from this wrongdoer` 100000 20 40 TRUE EQUALS 80000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can recover from this wrongdoer` 100000 0 100 FALSE EQUALS 100000
#ASSERT `What is the most I can recover from this wrongdoer` 50000 0 25 FALSE EQUALS 12500
-- s 32(2), (3): the duty arises only on every limb.
#ASSERT `Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the concurrent wrongdoer must give me, in writing, the information it has to help me identify and locate the other person, and why it believes that person is or may be a concurrent wrongdoer, as soon as practicable after it became aware of my claim or of the information, whichever is later (s 32(2), (3)); if it fails, the court may on application order it severally liable for any damages awarded, or to pay costs thrown away (s 32(5)); if I join the other person on that information and that person is found not liable, the court may make costs orders (s 32(6))`
#ASSERT `Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: s 32 applies only to a claim involving an apportionable claim (ss 28, 32(2))`
#ASSERT `Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the duty is on a concurrent wrongdoer in relation to my claim (s 32(2))`
-- The other person is already known to me: "not being a concurrent wrongdoer known to the claimant".
#ASSERT `Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the duty is only about another person the wrongdoer has reasonable grounds to believe is also a concurrent wrongdoer, and who is not a concurrent wrongdoer known to me (s 32(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the duty is only about another person the wrongdoer has reasonable grounds to believe is also a concurrent wrongdoer, and who is not a concurrent wrongdoer known to me (s 32(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Must the wrongdoer tell me of other wrongdoers` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the wrongdoer has no information likely to help me identify and locate that person (s 32(2))`
-- s 32B: loss $100,000; $32,000 recovered from the first wrongdoer leaves $68,000; all or more recovered leaves 0.
#ASSERT `What more can I recover from another wrongdoer` 100000 32000 EQUALS 68000
#ASSERT `What more can I recover from another wrongdoer` 100000 0 EQUALS 100000
#ASSERT `What more can I recover from another wrongdoer` 100000 100000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What more can I recover from another wrongdoer` 100000 120000 EQUALS 0

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 35-37`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can I sue the public authority` `failed to repair a road or keep it in repair, or to inspect it for that purpose, as road authority` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: a public or other authority is not liable for failing to repair or inspect a road, as it had no actual knowledge of the particular risk (s 37(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the public authority` `failed to repair a road or keep it in repair, or to inspect it for that purpose, as road authority` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 37 does not bar the claim: the authority had actual knowledge of the particular risk that caused my harm (s 37(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the public authority` `wrongly exercised, or failed to exercise, another function` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the act or omission was not so unreasonable that no authority with those functions could properly consider it reasonable, so it is not wrongful (s 36(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the public authority` `wrongly exercised, or failed to exercise, another function` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `s 36 does not bar the claim: the act or omission was so unreasonable that no authority could properly consider it a reasonable exercise of its functions (s 36(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the public authority` `something else` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `ss 36 and 37 do not apply; the court still applies the principles in s 35 about the authority's resources and functions`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 38-44`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the volunteer incurs no personal civil liability for what they did in good faith doing community work for a community organisation (s 39(1)); the Act does not say here whether the organisation is liable`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 39 does not protect: the person was not a volunteer doing community work on a voluntary basis (s 38)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 39 does not protect: the work was not organised by a community organisation, nor done as its office holder (s 39(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 39 does not protect: the act was not done in good faith (s 39(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer was engaged in conduct that is an offence (s 40)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer was intoxicated and failed to exercise due care and skill (s 41)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, the protection is lost: the volunteer knew or ought to have known they were acting outside the authorised activities or contrary to instructions (s 42)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `yes, the protection is lost: a written law requires the volunteer to be insured against the liability (s 43)`
-- A volunteer driver in a car crash: CTP covers it (s 44).
#ASSERT `Can I sue the volunteer personally` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes, the protection is lost: the liability is covered by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant (s 44)`
-- Food donors.
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `a food donor that donated or distributed it in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to me directly` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the donor incurs no civil liability for harm from consuming the food, which was safe when it left their possession, with any handling needs and time limits passed on (ss 38A, 39(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `a person who donated it to a community organisation in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the donor incurs no civil liability for harm from consuming the food, which was safe when it left their possession, with any handling needs and time limits passed on (ss 38A, 39(2))`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `the one who handed it to me directly, or someone else` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the protection does not apply: the person was not a food donor under s 38, nor a donor to a community organisation under s 39(3)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `a food donor that donated or distributed it in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to me directly` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the protection does not apply: the food was not safe to consume when it left the donor's possession`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `a food donor that donated or distributed it in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to me directly` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the protection does not apply: the donor did not pass on the handling requirements or time limit the food needed`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `a food donor that donated or distributed it in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to me directly` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `the protection does not apply: a written law requires the donor to be insured against the liability (ss 38B, 43)`
#ASSERT `Can I sue the food donor` `a food donor that donated or distributed it in good faith for a charitable or similar purpose, free to the consumer, not handing it to me directly` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the protection does not apply: the liability is covered by CTP insurance or recoverable from the Nominal Defendant (ss 38C, 44)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 45`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can I get damages although I was committing an offence` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the defendant incurs no civil liability, because I was engaged in an indictable offence that contributed materially to the risk (s 45(1))`
#ASSERT `Can I get damages although I was committing an offence` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `yes, but reduced: the court finds s 45(1) would operate harshly and unjustly, and my damages are reduced by at least 25% (s 45(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I get damages although I was committing an offence` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 45 does not bar my claim: my conduct did not contribute materially to the risk of the harm`
#ASSERT `Can I get damages although I was committing an offence` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 45 does not bar my claim: I was not engaged in conduct that is an indictable offence when the breach happened`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 47-49`
-- (arguments: my intoxication, alleged, did not contribute, not self-induced, driver, my BAC, incapable;
-- age, relied, aware, theirs did not contribute, could not avoid, passenger, their BAC, they incapable)
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- Nobody intoxicated: no minimum.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 0
-- s 47: I was drunk, a pedestrian, contributory negligence alleged, not rebutted: at least 25%.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 25
-- s 47(5): driver at 150mg/100mL: 50%; at 149: 25%; incapable of control whatever the reading: 50%.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 150 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 50
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 149 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 25
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 0 TRUE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 50
-- s 47(3): rebutted either way: no minimum.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 200 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE 200 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 0
-- s 47(1): contributory negligence not alleged.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE 200 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 0
-- s 48: aged 16, relied on a person known to be drunk: 25%; aged 15: no presumption.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 16 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 25
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 15 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 0
-- s 48(1)(c): not aware and ought not to have been.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS 0
-- s 49: their passenger, they at 150: 50%; at 149: 25%; they incapable: 50%.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 150 FALSE EQUALS 50
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 149 FALSE EQUALS 25
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 0 TRUE EQUALS 50
-- s 48(3): rebutted.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 200 FALSE EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE 200 FALSE EQUALS 0
-- Both: I was drunk (25%) and rode with a driver at 180 (50%): the larger, 50%.
#ASSERT `What is the least my damages are cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 180 FALSE EQUALS 50
#ASSERT `Why are my damages cut for intoxication` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted: my damages are reduced by at least 25% (ss 47(4), 48(4))`
#ASSERT `Why are my damages cut for intoxication` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 150 FALSE EQUALS `contributory negligence is presumed and not rebutted: my damages are reduced by at least 50%, the drink-driving minimum (ss 47(5), 49)`
#ASSERT `Why are my damages cut for intoxication` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE 30 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE EQUALS `no presumption of contributory negligence arises under ss 47-49; any reduction is for the court under the general law (and may be up to 100%, s 24)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 49A, 49B`
-- ===================================================================================================================

#ASSERT `Can I claim the costs of raising the child` `a sterilisation procedure (for example tubal ligation or vasectomy)` TRUE EQUALS `no: a court cannot award damages for economic loss from the costs ordinarily associated with rearing or maintaining the child (s 49A(2) or 49B(2)); other damages are not affected by this section`
#ASSERT `Can I claim the costs of raising the child` `a contraceptive procedure or contraceptive advice` TRUE EQUALS `no: a court cannot award damages for economic loss from the costs ordinarily associated with rearing or maintaining the child (s 49A(2) or 49B(2)); other damages are not affected by this section`
#ASSERT `Can I claim the costs of raising the child` `a sterilisation procedure (for example tubal ligation or vasectomy)` FALSE EQUALS `ss 49A and 49B do not apply: the child was not born or fathered after a failed sterilisation or contraceptive procedure or advice because of a breach of duty`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 68-72D`
-- (arguments: claim, personal injury, institution apology for child abuse, before proceeding, regret by
-- an individual)
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 72D for an ordinary claim, before or after the proceeding started.
#ASSERT `Can the apology be used against the defendant` `an ordinary claim for harm` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding fault or liability, and is not admissible as evidence of it (s 72D)`
-- s 72A(2)(c): an assault with intent to injure; part 1A does not apply, but an individual's expression of regret
-- before the proceeding is still inadmissible under s 72 (part 1 has no such exclusion).
#ASSERT `Can the apology be used against the defendant` `an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `no: the expression of regret, made before the proceeding started, is not admissible in the proceeding (s 72), except any part that admits liability (s 71)`
#ASSERT `Can the apology be used against the defendant` `an unlawful intentional act done with intent to cause personal injury` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not stop it: neither part 1 nor part 1A of chapter 4 covers this statement`
-- s 72A(3): an institution's apology about child abuse is protected despite (2)(d).
#ASSERT `Can the apology be used against the defendant` `an unlawful sexual assault or other unlawful sexual misconduct` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the apology is not an admission of fault or liability, is not relevant to deciding fault or liability, and is not admissible as evidence of it (s 72D)`
#ASSERT `Can the apology be used against the defendant` `defamation` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not stop it: neither part 1 nor part 1A of chapter 4 covers this statement`
-- s 72A(2)(a) with s 5: a workers' compensation injury claim; neither part reaches it.
#ASSERT `Can the apology be used against the defendant` `a personal injury claim the Act does not apply to under s 5 (for example workers' compensation, dust disease or tobacco)` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the Act does not stop it: neither part 1 nor part 1A of chapter 4 covers this statement`
